1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,720
our plans. They shall be carried out to the day.

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:10,760
Forward, comrades. Long live the windmill. Long live Animal Farm.

3
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:15,439
>> Chapter seven.

4
00:00:15,439 --> 00:00:17,560
>> It was a bitter winter.

5
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:23,279
The stormy weather was followed by sleet and snow and then by a hard frost which

6
00:00:23,279 --> 00:00:26,680
did not break till well into February.

7
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:30,800
The animals carried on as best they could with the rebuilding of the windmill

8
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:36,560
well knowing that the outside world was watching them and that the envious human beings would

9
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:41,239
rejoice and triumph if the mill were not finished on time.

10
00:00:41,239 --> 00:00:46,000
Out of spite, the human beings pretended not to believe that it was Snowball who

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:48,319
had destroyed the windmill.

12
00:00:48,319 --> 00:00:53,399
They said that it had fallen down because the walls were too thin.

13
00:00:53,399 --> 00:00:56,720
The animals knew that this was not the case.

14
00:00:56,720 --> 00:01:02,640
Still, it had been decided to build the walls 3 ft thick this time instead of 18

15
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:08,880
in as before which meant collecting much larger quantities of stone.

16
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:14,720
For a long time, the quarry was full of snowdrifts and nothing could be done.

17
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:20,479
Some progress was made in the dry frosty weather that followed, but it was cruel

18
00:01:20,479 --> 00:01:26,600
work, and the animals could not feel so hopeful about it as they had felt before.

19
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:30,880
They were always cold and usually hungry as well.

20
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:35,119
Only Boxer and Clover never lost heart.

21
00:01:35,119 --> 00:01:41,399
Squealer made excellent speeches on the joy of service and the dignity of labor,

22
00:01:41,399 --> 00:01:46,279
but the other animals found more inspiration in Boxer's strength and his

23
00:01:46,279 --> 00:01:52,319
never-failing cry of, "I will work harder."

24
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:55,560
In January, food fell short.

25
00:01:55,560 --> 00:01:59,880
The corn ration was drastically reduced, and it was announced that an extra

26
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:03,760
potato ration would be issued to make up for it.

27
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:07,520
Then it was discovered that the greater part of the potato crop had been frosted

28
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:11,640
in the clamps, which had not been covered thickly enough.

29
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:17,479
The potatoes had become soft and discolored, and only a few were edible.

30
00:02:17,479 --> 00:02:23,279
For days at a time, the animals had nothing to eat but chaff and mangels.

31
00:02:23,279 --> 00:02:26,560
Starvation seemed to stare them in the face.

32
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:31,960
It was vitally necessary to conceal this fact from the outside world.

33
00:02:31,960 --> 00:02:35,199
Emboldened by the collapse of the windmill, the human beings were

34
00:02:35,199 --> 00:02:38,640
inventing fresh lies about Animal Farm.

35
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:42,800
Once again, it was being put about that all the animals were dying of famine and

36
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:46,960
disease, and that they were continually fighting among themselves and had

37
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:51,119
resorted to cannibalism and infanticide.

38
00:02:51,119 --> 00:02:55,439
Napoleon was well aware of the bad results that might follow if the real

39
00:02:55,439 --> 00:03:00,039
facts of the food situation were known, and he decided to make use of Mr.

40
00:03:00,039 --> 00:03:03,800
Whymper to spread a contrary impression.

41
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:10,159
Hitherto, the animals had had little or no contact with Whymper on his weekly visits.

42
00:03:10,159 --> 00:03:16,399
Now, however, a few selected animals, mostly sheep, were instructed to remark

43
00:03:16,399 --> 00:03:20,880
casually in his hearing that rations had been increased.

44
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:25,680
In addition, Napoleon ordered the almost empty bins in the store shed to be

45
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:30,159
filled nearly to the brim with sand, which was then covered up with what

46
00:03:30,159 --> 00:03:33,159
remained of the grain and meal.

47
00:03:33,159 --> 00:03:37,680
On some suitable pretext, Whymper was led through the store shed and allowed

48
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:40,439
to catch a glimpse of the bins.

49
00:03:40,439 --> 00:03:45,399
He was deceived and continued to report to the outside world that there was no

50
00:03:45,399 --> 00:03:48,520
food shortage on Animal Farm.

51
00:03:48,520 --> 00:03:52,479
Nevertheless, towards the end of January, it became obvious that it would

52
00:03:52,479 --> 00:03:56,880
be necessary to procure some more grain from somewhere.

53
00:03:56,880 --> 00:04:01,560
In these days, Napoleon rarely appeared in public, but spent all his time in the

54
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:06,640
farmhouse, which was guarded at each door by fierce-looking dogs.

55
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:11,000
When he did emerge, it was in a ceremonial manner with an escort of six

56
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:16,800
dogs who closely surrounded him and growled if anyone came too near.

57
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:21,439
Frequently, he did not even appear on Sunday mornings, but issued his orders

58
00:04:21,439 --> 00:04:25,119
through one of the other pigs, usually Squealer.

59
00:04:25,119 --> 00:04:29,079
One Sunday morning, Squealer announced that the hens, who had just come in to

60
00:04:29,079 --> 00:04:33,199
lay again, must surrender their eggs.

61
00:04:33,199 --> 00:04:39,720
Napoleon had accepted, through Whymper, a contract for 400 eggs a week.

62
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:44,039
The price of these would pay for enough grain and meal to keep the farm going

63
00:04:44,039 --> 00:04:47,359
till summer came on and conditions were easier.

64
00:04:47,359 --> 00:04:51,880
When the hens heard this, they raised a terrible outcry.

65
00:04:51,880 --> 00:04:56,239
They had been warned earlier that this sacrifice might be necessary, but had

66
00:04:56,239 --> 00:04:59,159
not believed that it would really happen.

67
00:04:59,159 --> 00:05:02,560
They were just getting their clutches ready for the spring sitting, and they

68
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:07,560
protested that to take the eggs away now was murder.

69
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:14,199
For the first time since the expulsion of Jones, there was something resembling a rebellion.

70
00:05:14,199 --> 00:05:18,640
Led by three young black Minorca pullets, the hens made a determined

71
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:21,760
effort to thwart Napoleon's wishes.

72
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:26,279
Their method was to fly up to the rafters and there lay their eggs, which

73
00:05:26,279 --> 00:05:28,640
smashed to pieces on the floor.

74
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:31,920
Napoleon acted swiftly and ruthlessly.

75
00:05:31,920 --> 00:05:35,960
He ordered the hens' rations to be stopped and decreed that any animal

76
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:41,399
giving so much as a grain of corn to a hen should be punished by death.

77
00:05:41,399 --> 00:05:44,800
The dogs saw to it that these orders were carried out.

78
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:51,520
For 5 days the hens held out, then they capitulated and went back to their nesting boxes.

79
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:54,239
Nine hens had died in the meantime.

80
00:05:54,239 --> 00:05:58,000
Their bodies were buried in the orchard and it was given out that they had died
